        192.65625
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Affine shape warp. The SAME nine control points are fitted three ways, and the 'residual' output - where each control point actually landed minus where it was asked to land - is what tells the models apart. Top row: an exactly-affine target. The 6-dof 'full affine' reproduces it to the last bit (residual ~0, and the recovered 2x3 matrix IS the one that built the target); the 4-dof 'similarity' keeps angles and uniform scale, so it cannot represent the shear and reports a residual of tens of pixels instead of pretending. Bottom row: a target BENT the way a lens bends an image (a pull towards the centre growing with the SQUARE of the radius). No affine can reach it - the residual stays large however you fit it - while 'CV Thin Plate Spline Warp' interpolates it exactly, because a spline has one degree of freedom per control point. That is the whole choice: an affine is stiff and averages noisy matches, a spline is flexible and follows them (including the wrong ones)."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
